<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim</h1><p class="st-meta">856 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Bad Gandersheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A noble women's convent founded by the ancestors of the Ottonian emperors, home of Hrotsvitha, the first known German woman poet and playwright.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liudolf-duke-of-saxony">Liudolf</a> and his wife Oda founded a convent in 852 at Brunshausen, which moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-gandersheim">Gandersheim</a> in 856. Their daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathumod">Hathumod</a> was the first abbess. As the family monastery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Liudolfings</a>, who became the Ottonian kings, it enjoyed royal favour, and in 919 it was made a royal abbey.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-gandersheim">Gandersheim</a> was a community of canonesses, mostly from high noble families, ruled by an abbess who became a princess of the Empire. Around 960 the canoness <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrotsvitha-of-gandersheim">Hrotsvitha</a> wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> plays, legends and verse histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i-duke-of-bavaria">Otto I</a> there. The abbey ruled a small territory around Bad Gandersheim under the protection of the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-gandersheim">Gandersheim</a> accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1560s, becoming a Lutheran foundation under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> dukes. It lost its imperial status with secularisation in 1802 to 1803, passing to Brunswick, and the convent was dissolved in 1810 under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-gandersheim.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Misburg3014 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad-Gandersheim-Stiftskirche-Seite-vorn.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>856 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Gandersheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hathumod, Gerberga II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gandersheim Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1329&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-gandersheim" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4906 384 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Gandersheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5226 640L5229 694M5226 640L5237 632"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5237 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5226 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5226 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1329&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-gandersheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
